Okay, the question was,
who is the poorest in our group?

5
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:44,502
One vote for Thom.

6
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:46,671
- Sorry, bud.
- [Thom] Clearly Robert.

7
00:00:46,672 --> 00:00:48,423
Two votes for Thom.

8
00:00:50,259 --> 00:00:51,551
{\an8}Aw. Three votes for Thom.

9
00:00:52,177 --> 00:00:55,179
- Thom is officially the poorest.
- No, my dad abandoned us, okay?

10
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:57,640
He didn't give a grace period
for financial planning.

11
00:00:57,641 --> 00:01:00,685
He just fucking left with no warning. Glad
you're getting a good laugh out of it.

12
00:01:00,686 --> 00:01:03,229
- Thom, this is what we do.
- [Debbie chuckles]

13
00:01:03,230 --> 00:01:05,690
It's just us. It's always been us.

14
00:01:05,691 --> 00:01:08,609
It's purposefully silly and stupid.
It's just a game.

15
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:10,570
Yeah, a game that you always seem to win.

16
00:01:10,571 --> 00:01:12,947
Who's the prettiest? Who's the smartest?

17
00:01:12,948 --> 00:01:15,908
Most likely to succeed,
most likely to win homecoming?

18
00:01:15,909 --> 00:01:17,994
Susan, Susan, Susan, Susan.

19
00:01:17,995 --> 00:01:20,621
Meanwhile, this guy right here sweeps
who's the poorest,

20
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:23,040
and Debbie routinely gets
most who needs to drop ten.

21
00:01:23,041 --> 00:01:27,629
Excuse me, I won that one time
after eating a bag of Doritos.

22
00:01:28,839 --> 00:01:31,258
Oh, my God, Bret, where have you been?

23
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:34,468
I've been calling you all day.
You didn't pick up.

24
00:01:34,469 --> 00:01:35,970
[Robert] Yes, where have you been, Bret?

25
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:39,265
You look tired and paler than usual,
which is saying something.

26
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:41,267
[Susan] Leave him alone. He's here now.

27
00:01:41,268 --> 00:01:43,478
Bret, we're playing secret ballot.
You do the question.

28
00:01:44,062 --> 00:01:45,146
Who is The Trawler?

29
00:01:45,147 --> 00:01:47,357
[Debbie] No, not sexy enough.

30
00:01:47,858 --> 00:01:52,695
I prefer something like, uh, best tits,
which I would sweep.

31
00:01:52,696 --> 00:01:54,990
But as gatekeeper and vote counter,
I'll accept.

32
00:01:55,866 --> 00:01:58,368
Bret, it's vote time. Vote.

33
00:02:00,412 --> 00:02:04,290
[Robert] I win too. Best-looking,
most likely to have the biggest dick,

34
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:06,709
best breath, sexiest treasure trail.

35
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:09,545
Hey, that one was mine. I loved that one.

36
00:02:09,546 --> 00:02:11,297
Best ass, best smile.

37
00:02:11,298 --> 00:02:13,300
- [Debbie] Okay.
- [Robert] I could go on and on.

38
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:19,389
Uh-oh, one vote for Bret.

39
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:23,809
Debbie, that's clearly your handwriting.

40
00:02:23,810 --> 00:02:26,521
What can I say?
You've been obsessed with death lately.

41
00:02:27,189 --> 00:02:30,901
One for Riders of the Afterlife.
Oh. Is that that cult?

42
00:02:31,735 --> 00:02:32,736
Yawn.

43
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,404
One for Really Rhonda.

44
00:02:35,405 --> 00:02:36,822
[Susan] My vote, obviously.

45
00:02:36,823 --> 00:02:39,992
I used to like her, as you all know,
but now I know she's deranged.

46
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:42,161
Saying I was a kidnapper
and a perpetrator of violence

47
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:44,455
in front of everyone
when I graciously gave up my crown.

48
00:02:44,456 --> 00:02:45,915
[Debbie] Hon, you're a legend now.

49
00:02:45,916 --> 00:02:48,501
You spent 45 minutes in jail
and were then released.

50
00:02:48,502 --> 00:02:50,753
Still, my parents had to come.

51
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:52,631
My mom lost her mind.

52
00:02:53,256 --> 00:02:55,591
So much crying, it was mortifying.
You weren't there.

53
00:02:55,592 --> 00:02:59,553
Who cares? Little Miss March of the Dimes
recanted it instantly,

54
00:02:59,554 --> 00:03:01,430
said she was confused.

55
00:03:01,431 --> 00:03:04,725
Her parents took her
to some loony bin in Tennessee,

56
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:06,811
where she always should have been.

57
00:03:06,812 --> 00:03:10,314
Your rep's at an all-time high, Su Su.
Why so bitter?

58
00:03:10,315 --> 00:03:11,565
You're right.

59
00:03:11,566 --> 00:03:14,528
Rhonda's disturbed.
She's been through so much.

60
00:03:16,363 --> 00:03:19,782
I would like to publicly recant
my secret ballot vote and then abstain.

61
00:03:19,783 --> 00:03:21,451
I'm sorry, I'm still a bit shaky.

62
00:03:22,035 --> 00:03:25,246
[Debbie] Okay, one vote for Robert.

63
00:03:25,247 --> 00:03:28,332
Hey, two votes for Bret, the winner.

64
00:03:28,333 --> 00:03:31,043
- Looks like you're The Trawler, Bret.
- [Debbie] Whoo! [claps]

65
00:03:31,044 --> 00:03:32,671
- Looks like.
- [Debbie chuckles]

66
00:03:34,089 --> 00:03:35,673
Hey, do you guys want to know

67
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:38,050
why you couldn't get ahold of me
for the last 18 hours?

68
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:39,052
[Debbie] Obviously.

69
00:03:40,512 --> 00:03:44,098
I was at the police station all night
giving testimony.

70
00:03:44,099 --> 00:03:46,184
[Susan] What? Why?

71
00:03:49,354 --> 00:03:51,106
Matt Kellner is dead.

72
00:03:55,068 --> 00:03:56,486
Holy shit.

73
00:03:58,071 --> 00:03:59,656
I found his severed head.

74
00:04:04,619 --> 00:04:06,496
Somebody wanted me to find it.

75
00:04:07,038 --> 00:04:08,373
[♪ ominous music plays]

76
00:04:17,299 --> 00:04:19,676
[Bret]
<i>Matt Kellner's death changed everything.</i>

77
00:04:20,844 --> 00:04:22,387
{\an8}[radio chatter]

78
00:04:24,890 --> 00:04:27,726
<i>I was no longer the only concerned party.</i>

79
00:04:28,852 --> 00:04:31,395
<i>Every single person
who had accused me of paranoia</i>

80
00:04:31,396 --> 00:04:34,733
<i>could no longer deny
that there was a killer in our midst.</i>

81
00:04:35,609 --> 00:04:37,276
[camera shutter clicks]

82
00:04:37,277 --> 00:04:40,154
<i>School was closed for one week,
and when it reopened,</i>

83
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:43,450
<i>there was a sudden police presence
that would last through the entire year.</i>

84
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:48,455
<i>The interrogations began.</i>

85
00:04:49,706 --> 00:04:51,541
<i>Everyone was suddenly a suspect.</i>

86
00:04:53,251 --> 00:04:54,335
[school bell rings]

87
00:04:54,336 --> 00:04:56,212
<i>If you're wondering
how The Trawler got away</i>

88
00:04:56,213 --> 00:04:59,548
<i>with infiltrating a high school campus
on the night of homecoming,</i>

89
00:04:59,549 --> 00:05:02,760
<i>you must remember, this was 1981.</i>

90
00:05:02,761 --> 00:05:05,387
<i>There were no cell phones,
no surveillance cameras,</i>

91
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:07,848
<i>no digital records, no metal detectors.</i>

92
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:09,434
<i>That was all post 9/11.</i>

93
00:05:10,143 --> 00:05:14,523
<i>It was just people then.
People fumbling and failing.</i>

94
00:05:19,444 --> 00:05:22,071
<i>We returned to normal life
as best we could,</i>

95
00:05:22,072 --> 00:05:24,533
<i>with the hope
that feelings of normalcy would follow.</i>

96
00:05:26,409 --> 00:05:29,079
- Nice of you to join us, Mr. Ellis.
- I'm sorry.

97
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:36,545
Why are you in my seat?

98
00:05:37,587 --> 00:05:38,755
I don't know. I just sat here.

99
00:05:41,675 --> 00:05:43,260
Why is everybody in different seats?

100
00:05:43,844 --> 00:05:46,178
Mystery for another day, Mr. Ellis.

101
00:05:46,179 --> 00:05:47,472
Just sit anywhere.

102
00:05:48,598 --> 00:05:52,017
A kidnapping, a murder
and an active police investigation,

103
00:05:52,018 --> 00:05:53,728
and it's not even Halloween yet.

104
00:05:54,229 --> 00:05:58,566
A lot of people in my position
would probably switch up the curriculum,

105
00:05:58,567 --> 00:05:59,568
coddle you,

106
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:05,574
but I am not going to do that here
in senior class, Shakespeare 101.

107
00:06:06,241 --> 00:06:09,869
<i>Romeo and Juliet</i> is a play about

108
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:13,999
death, obsession, angst and love.

109
00:06:14,583 --> 00:06:17,419
It's about
what it feels like to be a teenager.

110
00:06:18,128 --> 00:06:23,173
Act 1, scene 3, Juliet is not yet 14,

111
00:06:23,174 --> 00:06:25,552
and Romeo's probably a little bit older.

112
00:06:26,636 --> 00:06:29,514
A 13-year-old and a 15-year-old,

113
00:06:30,098 --> 00:06:32,893
an eighth grader and a sophomore.

114
00:06:35,186 --> 00:06:37,188
Miss Reynolds, you're making a face.

115
00:06:38,231 --> 00:06:40,649
Eighth graders don't understand love.

116
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:43,277
They're not even mature enough
for off-campus lunch.

117
00:06:43,278 --> 00:06:45,738
Fair point. What about sophomores?

118
00:06:45,739 --> 00:06:47,323
Romeo's just about your age.

119
00:06:47,324 --> 00:06:50,117
Yeah, teenagers think they're in love,
but it goes away.

120
00:06:50,118 --> 00:06:52,161
In the beginning, it's so intense,

121
00:06:52,162 --> 00:06:55,497
and then it fades
or it disappears overnight.

122
00:06:55,498 --> 00:06:57,291
And then all that's left is habit.

123
00:06:57,292 --> 00:06:58,792
That's bullshit.

124
00:06:58,793 --> 00:07:00,961
What difference does it make
how old they are?

125
00:07:00,962 --> 00:07:03,797
[Susan] Their brains are underdeveloped
and flooded with hormones.

126
00:07:03,798 --> 00:07:06,467
- Thom did the reading? Is he okay?
- [Thom] That means they can't love?

127
00:07:06,468 --> 00:07:08,677
[Susan] Real love shouldn't make you
want to kill yourself.

128
00:07:08,678 --> 00:07:11,305
The only thing Romeo and Juliet killed
was themselves.

129
00:07:11,306 --> 00:07:12,307
That makes sense to me.

130
00:07:13,141 --> 00:07:14,475
[Ms. Rogers] Speak on that.

131
00:07:14,476 --> 00:07:16,935
If you're in love and you kill yourself,
that's dying once.

132
00:07:16,936 --> 00:07:19,188
If you're in love and you can't be
with the person you love,

133
00:07:19,189 --> 00:07:20,482
that's like dying every day.

134
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:25,819
Dying once, dying every day.

135
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:28,947
Is it?
Is it actually like dying every day?

136
00:07:28,948 --> 00:07:32,451
Or would they both have gotten over it
as soon as they met someone new?

137
00:07:32,452 --> 00:07:35,996
How many people do you know who break up,
act like it's the end of the world,

138
00:07:35,997 --> 00:07:39,041
and then by seventh period
have a new life-defining crush?

139
00:07:39,042 --> 00:07:41,127
Romeo and Juliet should have moved on.

140
00:07:41,753 --> 00:07:45,924
It's not the most romantic take,
Miss Reynolds, but I like it.

141
00:07:46,883 --> 00:07:50,094
There's an open space
in our divorced educator's book club,

142
00:07:50,095 --> 00:07:53,138
- if you're in.
- You're wrong. They're soulmates.

143
00:07:53,139 --> 00:07:56,975
Juliet's special.
She's not some eighth-grade slut.

144
00:07:56,976 --> 00:07:57,977
She's an angel.

145
00:07:59,062 --> 00:08:00,479
Angels aren't sluts.

146
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:02,649
[Ms. Rogers] Angels aren't sluts.

147
00:08:04,275 --> 00:08:08,904
That sounds like a very good area
to explore in your paper, Mr. Wright.

148
00:08:08,905 --> 00:08:10,448
[♪ dramatic music plays]

149
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:27,549
- [♪ pop music plays on speakers]
- [clears throat]

150
00:08:35,432 --> 00:08:37,266
- What?
- Nothing.

151
00:08:37,267 --> 00:08:38,393
You're staring at me.

152
00:08:38,893 --> 00:08:41,478
I'm not allowed to look at my girlfriend
while we do our homework?

153
00:08:41,479 --> 00:08:43,481
You can look anywhere you want.
I don't care.

154
00:08:45,025 --> 00:08:46,401
I just think it's really sad.

155
00:08:48,111 --> 00:08:49,862
What's really sad, Thom?

156
00:08:49,863 --> 00:08:52,407
You don't think Romeo and Juliet
are meant to be together.

157
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:55,617
The play is called <i>Romeo and Juliet.</i>

158
00:08:55,618 --> 00:08:58,287
So what?
There's a cartoon called <i>Tom and Jerry</i>.

159
00:08:58,288 --> 00:09:00,165
Do you think they're meant
to be together too?

160
00:09:01,833 --> 00:09:02,834
Fine, Thom.

161
00:09:03,877 --> 00:09:06,920
They are perfect for each other, and
we'll just have to take your word for it

162
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:10,341
because we'll never know, because Romeo
and Juliet killed themselves.

163
00:09:10,925 --> 00:09:14,428
Exactly. That proves how much
they loved each other.

164
00:09:14,429 --> 00:09:16,972
Maybe they weren't supposed
to be together forever.

165
00:09:16,973 --> 00:09:19,183
Maybe it was only supposed to be
a Verona thing.

166
00:09:19,184 --> 00:09:20,434
A Verona thing?

167
00:09:20,435 --> 00:09:22,352
Yeah. And then once they leave Verona…

168
00:09:22,353 --> 00:09:23,979
Why would they leave Verona?

169
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:26,107
- To see other places.
- Like what?

170
00:09:27,984 --> 00:09:31,862
Because even now, like in modern times,
there's only so many places in the world,

171
00:09:31,863 --> 00:09:33,822
if you think about it, maybe a hundred.

172
00:09:33,823 --> 00:09:35,199
What are you talking about?

173
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:38,577
Like, sure, Toledo is a place,
but you're not gonna, like, go there.

174
00:09:38,578 --> 00:09:41,413
So for all intents and purposes,
it doesn't exist.

175
00:09:41,414 --> 00:09:44,209
Do you have any curiosity
in the outside world, Thom?

176
00:09:44,709 --> 00:09:47,211
Do you care about anything
besides the things

177
00:09:47,212 --> 00:09:49,630
that you personally experience every day?

178
00:09:49,631 --> 00:09:51,299
No. Do you?

179
00:09:51,883 --> 00:09:53,051
[chuckles] Yes.

180
00:09:54,177 --> 00:09:56,762
So, what, you care about Toledo?

181
00:09:56,763 --> 00:09:59,723
Sure, yeah,
and I care about Rome and Paris--

182
00:09:59,724 --> 00:10:02,227
You care about Toledo
more than you care about me?

183
00:10:03,770 --> 00:10:07,315
Susan, do you care about Toledo
more than you care about me?

184
00:10:08,358 --> 00:10:10,068
This is deranged. I'm not doing this.

185
00:10:11,277 --> 00:10:14,613
And I'm not correcting your paper
or writing it for you like I always do.

186
00:10:14,614 --> 00:10:16,156
Finish this assignment yourself.

187
00:10:16,157 --> 00:10:18,909
I love you, Susan.
I would die without you.

188
00:10:18,910 --> 00:10:20,619
Thom, that's too much pressure.

189
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:23,038
I don't want to be with someone
who needs me to survive.

190
00:10:23,039 --> 00:10:25,082
You are the center of my universe.
You're my Earth.

191
00:10:25,083 --> 00:10:28,752
That's the sun.
The sun is the center of the universe.

192
00:10:28,753 --> 00:10:30,255
"Juliet is the sun.

193
00:10:31,798 --> 00:10:33,466
Arise, fair sun,

194
00:10:34,134 --> 00:10:36,094
and kill the envious moon,

195
00:10:36,761 --> 00:10:40,265
who is already sick and pale with grief

196
00:10:40,807 --> 00:10:44,561
that thou, her maid,
are far more fair than she."

197
00:10:46,104 --> 00:10:47,939
"Oh, swear not by the moon,

198
00:10:48,606 --> 00:10:54,027
the inconstant moon
that monthly changes in her circle orb,

199
00:10:54,028 --> 00:10:57,157
lest that thy love prove
likewise variable."

200
00:10:59,284 --> 00:11:00,451
Thom,

201
00:11:01,786 --> 00:11:03,246
I need some space.

202
00:11:05,623 --> 00:11:08,835
[stammers] What does that mean?

203
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:12,546
I think we should break up.

204
00:11:12,547 --> 00:11:14,047
Did Robert put you up to this?

205
00:11:14,048 --> 00:11:15,424
Robert?

206
00:11:15,425 --> 00:11:17,593
He's manipulating you. Bret's right.

207
00:11:17,594 --> 00:11:20,345
Ever since he got here,
you've been a completely different person.

208
00:11:20,346 --> 00:11:23,891
